      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate fragment.\n\nCertificate details:\n- Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03 revision 11\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 4141724\n- Page total: 7\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 28 July 2026\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\nIssuing authority:\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- Address: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\nApplicant:\n- Badger Meter, Inc., 4545 West Brown Deer Road, Milwaukee, WI 53224, United States of America\n\nManufacturer:\n- Badger Meter Czech Republic s.r.o., Maříkova 2082/26, 62100 Brno, Czech Republic\n\nCertified type:\n- Category: Water meter (electromagnetic water meter)\n- Type designations: M5000\n- Description: An electromagnetic water meter\n\nRecommendation:\n- R 49-1 (2013), edition 2013 → edition should be int... \"R 49-1 (2013)\" — edition 2013? The recommendation is R49-1 with edition 2013. Hmm, edition as int: 2013. Amendment: null. Scheme: A. Accuracy classes: [1, 2]\n\nTest reports:\n- NMi-13200198-01 revision 1, dated 7 July 2022, 29 pages, role: type_evaluation_report? The text says \"The conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". The documentation folder is T10554-7. The first report likely is the Type Evaluation Report. Hard to tell roles. I'll assign roles: the reports are test/evaluation reports. Given the schema role options: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. Since we don't know exactly, I could mark them all as test_report, but the first might be the type evaluation report. The cert says \"represented by the sample(s) identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report\" — the reports listed include the TER. Safer: mark first as type_evaluation_report, rest as test_report? Actually, we can't be sure. Let me keep them all as \"test_report\" except maybe first one... Hmm. The instruction says don't fabricate. Role is a required field per schema. I'll use \"test_report\" for all, and maybe the first as \"type_evaluation_report\" — risky. Actually many NMi certs: the first listed report is the Type Evaluation Report. But revision 11 with many amendments... I'll just label all as \"test_report\" to be safe. Actually, \"associated reports\" — could be evaluation_report. I'll use \"test_report\".\n\nWait, documentation folder T10554-7 is mentioned: \"The construction of the measuring instrument is recorded in the Documentation folder no. T10554-7.\" That could be a test_reports entry with role documentation_file? The schema says test_reports[] with role: documentation_file is allowed. I could include it: id \"T10554-7\", role \"documentation_file\". Hmm, it's a documentation folder — yes, include it as documentation_file.\n\nCharacteristics:\n\nTable 1 General characteristics (type_level):\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic flow metering\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" → value string? Could be value: [1,2]? Schema wants value number/string/range. I'll use value: \"1 and 2\" as string, or maybe accuracy_classes in recommendation covers it. For type_level, use value: \"1 and 2\". Hmm, better: value: [1, 2]? Not allowed by schema shape (number | {min,max} | string). Use string \"1 and 2\".\n- environmental_class: \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\" → value string \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\". Maybe split: environmental_class \"M1/O\". I'll keep string.\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50 (+0.1 °C / +50 °C)\" → value string \"T50\" plus maybe separate. Keep string: \"T50 (+0.1 °C / +50 °C)\". Convert commas? There are no commas in decimals here except \"0,1\" → 0.1. Keep string.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa (16 bar). Value: 1.6, unit \"MPa\"; could add secondary. I'll do value 1.6 unit_symbol MPa, and maybe a separate characteristic maximum_admissible_pressure_bar: 16, unit \"bar\". Or keep string \"1.6 MPa (16 bar)\". Better structured: two entries.\n- orientation: \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" → maybe value boolean-ish string. Keep string or value: true. I'll use string.\n- pressure_loss_class: two values: full bore Δp 10 (0.10 bar), reduced bore Δp 40 (0.40 bar). This is a config-level characteristic! Axis: bore type. Use config_level:\n  - attribute: pressure_loss_class, axis: bore, values: [{condition: \"full bore\", value: 10... }]. Hmm, the class Δp 10 corresponds to 0.10 bar. I could do:\n    - attribute: \"pressure_loss\", axis: \"bore\", values: [{condition: \"full bore\", value: {min null max 0.1}? unit bar}]. Actually Δp 10 means pressure loss class with max 0.10 bar... For R49, pressure-loss classes Δp 10, Δp 16, etc. correspond to maximum pressure loss of 0.10 bar, 0.16 bar... Actually Δp 10 = 0.10 bar? Given the cert says \"Δp 10 (0,10 bar)\", value = 0.10 bar max. I'll record:\n    - config_level: attribute \"pressure_loss\", axis \"bore\", values: [{condition: \"full bore\", value: 0.1, unit \"bar\"}, {condition: \"reduced bore\", value: 0.4, unit \"bar\"}]\n  Also pressure_loss_class values: \"Δp 10\" and \"Δp 40\". Maybe two entries: pressure_loss_class and pressure_loss. I'll include both? Keep it simpler: one config_level for pressure_loss with class noted? I'll do two config entries: pressure_loss_class and maximum_pressure_loss.\n- power_supply: \"Internal or external replaceable battery (2,9 – 3,7 V)\" → characteristic battery_voltage: {min 2.9, max 3.7}, unit V; power_supply_type: \"internal or external replaceable battery\".\n- software_identification: \"See table 5\" → omit or string. I'll include as software_identification: \"See table 5\"? It's a pointer, not a value. Omit. Actually could include string value \"See table 5\". Rules say don't fabricate; the value present is \"See table 5\". I'll omit it since it's a cross-reference, not a characteristic. Hmm, it is a characteristic entry though. I'll include with string value \"See table 5\". Better to omit — I think including with string is fine but noisy. I'll omit.\n\nTables 2 & 3: flow rates by meter size for accuracy class 1 and class 2. This is a model matrix. Models: DN50...DN600 (class 1), plus class 2 has reduced bore variants.\n\nHow to model? The models differ per accuracy class. Options:\n- model_family.models with model_id like \"DN50 (class 1)\", \"DN50 (class 2)\", \"DN50 RB (class 2)\"...\n- model_level attributes: diameter (mm), q1, q2, q3, q4 (m³/h), ratio_q3_q1.\n- config axis could be accuracy class! Actually accuracy class is a configuration axis: same meter size can be class 1 or class 2 with different flow rates. And reduced bore is another axis.\n\nPer rule 5, config_level: attribute × config-axis matrix. So:\n- model_level: meter size models DN50–DN600.\n- config_level: axis \"accuracy_class\" for q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio.\n\nBut reduced bore variants are also config (bore type) with different q1 and ratio. Combined axes: accuracy class × bore. The class-2 table has both standard and reduced bore rows. Class 1 table has no reduced bore.\n\nApproach: model_family models = meter sizes DN50...DN600 (14 sizes). Then model_level: nominal_diameter (mm). config_level: attributes q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1 with axis \"accuracy_class/bore\" and conditions like \"class 1\", \"class 2\", \"class 2 reduced bore\".\n\nThat's clean. Conditions:\n- q1: class 1: DN50 0.315 ... per size; class 2: different values; class 2 RB: different values.\n\nBut config_level values structure: {condition, value}. Since value varies per model too, this becomes a 2-D matrix (size × class). Hmm. That's what matrix_tables is for. I'll emit matrix_tables for Table 2 and Table 3 preserving rows, AND populate model_level with nominal_diameter, and config_level for class-dependent attributes with conditions per class, where value varies... but config_level value is a single value per condition — can't encode 14 sizes.\n\nAlternative approach: treat each (size, class, bore) as a model variant. Models: DN50-c1, DN50-c2, DN50-c2-RB, ... That's 14 + 18 + ... class1: 14 sizes; class2: 18 rows (14 standard + 4 RB? let me count: DN50, DN50 RB, DN65, DN65 RB, DN80, DN80 RB, DN100, DN100 RB, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600 = 18 rows). Total 32 variants.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n- nominal_diameter_mm per model\n- q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1 per model\n- accuracy_class per model\n- bore per model\n\nThat's a lot but correct. Given \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only\", I should be thorough. But output size matters. 32 models × 7 attributes... the values arrays would be 32 entries each. That's fine.\n\nAlternatively, use config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\" — but then value per condition is ambiguous across sizes. I think the cleanest per schema: model_id per (size, class, bore) variant.\n\nHmm, but maybe simpler: model_id = \"DN50\" etc., and config axis \"accuracy_class\" with conditions \"class 1\" and \"class 2\" — but values differ per size, so config_level can't hold it. So model variants it is.\n\nLet me name model_ids:\nClass 1: \"DN50 (class 1)\", \"DN65 (class 1)\", ..., \"DN600 (class 1)\"\nClass 2: \"DN50 (class 2)\", \"DN50 RB (class 2)\", etc.\n\nActually the reduced bore identification is \"DNxxx RB\". So model_id \"DN50 RB (class 2)\".\n\nLet me tabulate Table 2 (class 1):\n- DN50: Ø50, Q1 0.315, Q2 0.504, Q3 63, Q4 78.75, ratio 200\n- DN65: 65, 0.5, 0.8, 100, 125, 200\n- DN80: 80, 0.8, 1.28, 160, 200, 200\n- DN100: 100, 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n- DN125: 125, 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250\n- DN150: 150, 3.9375, 6.3, 630, 787.5, 160\n- DN200: 200, 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\n- DN250: 250, 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n- DN300: 300, 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n- DN350: 350, 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n- DN400: 400, 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160\n- DN450: 450, 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n- DN500: 500, 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n- DN600: 600, 50.4, 80.64, 6300, 7875, 125\n\nTable 3 (class 2):\n- DN50: 50, 0.315, 0.504, 63, 78.75, 200\n- DN50 RB: 50, 0.158, 0.25, 63, 78.75, 400\n- DN65: 65, 0.5, 0.8, 100, 125, 200\n- DN65 RB: 65, 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n- DN80: 80, 0.8, 1.28, 160, 200, 200\n- DN80 RB: 80, 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n- DN100: 100, 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n- DN100 RB: 100, 0.63, 1.00, 250, 312.5, 400\n- DN125: 125, 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250\n- DN150: 150, 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\n- DN200: 200, 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\n- DN250: 250, 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\n- DN300: 300, 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n- DN350: 350, 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n- DN400: 400, 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\n- DN450: 450, 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n- DN500: 500, 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n- DN600: 600, 40, 64, 10000, 12500, 250\n\nFootnote: \"* For the reduced bore (RB) version...\" → footnotes[] with marker \"*\".\n\nAlso model_level attribute \"accuracy_class\" per model: 1 or 2. And \"bore\": \"full\" / \"reduced\". I'll include nominal_diameter_mm, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, accuracy_class, bore. That's 8 model_level attributes × 32 models. Large but okay.\n\nActually, to reduce noise, maybe skip accuracy_class and bore as model_level attributes and encode in model_id + attributes{} in model_family.models. The schema: models[] entries have model_id, label, attributes{}. I can put accuracy_class and bore in attributes{} of each model. Then model_level holds diameter and flow attributes. That's nicer.\n\nLet me build:\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"M5000\" (family of instruments). The cert says \"Tables 2 – 4 give an overview of the general characteristics of the family of instruments.\" Family name: \"M5000\"? The type is M5000. I'll set family_name: \"M5000\".\n- models: 32 entries:\n  - {model_id: \"DN50\", label: \"DN50 (accuracy class 1)\", attributes: {accuracy_class: 1, bore: \"full\"}}\n  etc.\n\nHmm, but model_id duplicated across classes. Better model_id unique: \"DN50-C1\", ... Or \"DN50 (class 1)\". I'll use \"DN50 (class 1)\" style as model_id to be unique and readable.\n\nmatrix_tables: two tables:\n1. name: \"General characteristics of the family of instruments with accuracy class 1\", columns: [\"meter_size\", \"diameter_inlet_outlet\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\"], column_units: [null, \"mm\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null], rows: 14 rows.\n2. name: \"General characteristics of the family of instruments with accuracy class 2\", same columns, 18 rows, footnotes: [\"* For the reduced bore (RB) version, the size mentioned is the process connection size. On the inscriptions of the water meter the reduced bore meter will be identified as \"DNxxx RB\".\"]\n\ncharacteristics.type_level (Table 1):\n- measuring_principle: {value: \"Electromagnetic flow metering\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null}\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"1 and 2\"} — or omit since recommendation has accuracy_classes. I'll include: value [1,2]? Not allowed. Use string \"1 and 2\".\n- environmental_class: {value: \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\"}\n- electromagnetic_environment: {value: \"E2\"}\n- temperature_range_ambient: {value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\", ...} plus temperature range? The entry: \"T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C)\". I'll do water_temperature_class: value \"T50\", and water_temperature_range: {min 0.1, max 50}, unit °C. The lower bound is +0.1 °C. So {min: 0.1, max: 50}.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"} and max admissible pressure bar: {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}. Two separate entries? Or one with value 1.6 MPa. I'll add both: maximum_admissible_pressure (MPa, 1.6) and maximum_admissible_pressure_alt (bar, 16)? The label \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" once; secondary in parentheses. I'll include second as \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\". Hmm, could be noisy but informative. I'll include it.\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\"}\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: {value: \"U0 and D0\"} plus maybe straight_length requirement. I'll add flow_profile_sensitivity_class value \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\". Keep full string.\n- reverse_flow: {value: true}? The text \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\". I'll use value: true with attribute reverse_flow_capable? Just keep attribute reverse_flow, value \"intended to measure reverse flow\"? I'll use {value: true} for reverse_flow. Hmm, fabricating boolean. Keep string: value: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\". Slightly long but faithful. I'll use value: true... no — don't fabricate. Use string.\n- pressure_loss: config_level with axis \"bore\":\n  - {condition: \"full bore\", value: 0.1, unit_symbol: \"bar\"} — wait the table says \"Full bore meter Δp 10 (0,10 bar)\". So pressure loss class Δp 10, pressure loss 0.10 bar.\n  - {condition: \"reduced bore\", value: 0.4, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n  Also pressure_loss_class config: {condition: \"full bore\", value: \"Δp 10\"}, {condition: \"reduced bore\", value: \"Δp 40\"}. I'll include both config_level entries.\n- power_supply_type: {value: \"Internal or external replaceable battery\"}\n- battery_voltage: {value: {min: 2.9, max: 3.7}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n\nFootnote markers: none in Table 1. Values don't have footnotes here. The RB footnote marker \"*\" applies to Table 3 rows.\n\nTest reports roles: The fragment says \"associated reports\". The last ones dated 2025 are probably revision reports. I'll set role \"test_report\" for all listed. First one NMi-13200198-01 might be the TER. Without explicit info, \"test_report\" is safe for all. Actually \"type_evaluation_report\" — the certificate references \"the OIML Type Evaluation Report\" — one of these is it. Can't determine which. All \"test_report\".\n\nDocumentation folder T10554-7 → test_reports entry: {id: \"T10554-7\", role: \"documentation_file\"}? It says \"Documentation folder no. T10554-7\". Include with role documentation_file, no date/pages.\n\nRevision history: \"revision 11\" but no history given. Omit revision_history.\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"4141724\"\n- page_total: 7\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2026-07-28\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\"\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n- person_title: null / omit (not given; \"Certification Board\" is a signature block label, not title). Omit.\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\" (canonical). Actually R49-1 — part 1. Canonical id \"R49\", edition 2013. Should I include part? id: \"R49-1\"? The rule says canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". R49-1 is a part of R49. I'll use \"R49-1\"? Hmm. \"R 49-1 (2013)\" — the recommendation is R 49, part 1. I'd put id \"R49-1\" to preserve part info. I think \"R49-1\" is reasonable. Edition: 2013 (int). Amendment: null — omit. scheme: \"A\". accuracy_classes: [1, 2].\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"M5000\"]\n- description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\"\n- module_designation: omit/null (not applicable; not stated). Omit.\n\nNow decimals: convert 0,315 → 0.315, 0,504 → 0.504, 78,75 → 78.75, 312,5 → 312.5, 3,9375 → 3.9375, 39,375 → 39.375, 50,400 → 50.4, 80,640 → 80.64, 2,52 → 2.52, 4,032 → 4.032, 6,4 → 6.4, 10,24 → 10.24, 25,2 → 25.2, 40,32 → 40.32, 1,6 → 1.6, 2,56 → 2.56, 2,9–3,7 → 2.9–3.7, 0,10 → 0.1, 0,40 → 0.4, 0,1 °C → 0.1.\n\nModel_level arrays: attributes q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, nominal_diameter. 32 values each. Let me generate.\n\nModel IDs: I'll use:\nClass 1: \"DN50 (class 1)\", \"DN65 (class 1)\", \"DN80 (class 1)\", \"DN100 (class 1)\", \"DN125 (class 1)\", \"DN150 (class 1)\", \"DN200 (class 1)\", \"DN250 (class 1)\", \"DN300 (class 1)\", \"DN350 (class 1)\", \"DN400 (class 1)\", \"DN450 (class 1)\", \"DN500 (class 1)\", \"DN600 (class 1)\"\nClass 2: \"DN50 (class 2)\", \"DN50 RB (class 2)\", \"DN65 (class 2)\", \"DN65 RB (class 2)\", \"DN80 (class 2)\", \"DN80 RB (class 2)\", \"DN100 (class 2)\", \"DN100 RB (class 2)\", \"DN125 (class 2)\", \"DN150 (class 2)\", \"DN200 (class 2)\", \"DN250 (class 2)\", \"DN300 (class 2)\", \"DN350 (class 2)\", \"DN400 (class 2)\", \"DN450 (class 2)\", \"DN500 (class 2)\", \"DN600 (class 2)\"\n\nmodels[] attributes: {accuracy_class: 1, bore: \"full\"} etc. For RB: bore: \"reduced\".\n\nNow model_level entries:\n\nnominal_diameter (meter_size encoded in id) — I'll include \"meter_size\" as model_level? It's in the id already. Skip; include diameter_inlet_outlet_mm instead (Ø in- and outlet). \n\nmodel_level:\n1. diameter_inlet_outlet: unit \"mm\", values per model.\n2. q1 (minimum_flow_rate): unit \"m³/h\"\n3. q2 (transitional_flow_rate): unit \"m³/h\"\n4. q3 (permanent_flow_rate): unit \"m³/h\"\n5. q4 (overload_flow_rate): unit \"m³/h\"\n6. ratio_q3_q1: dimensionless.\n\nAttribute naming: use q1/q2/q3/q4 canonical: \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\". The R137 example uses qmax etc. So \"q1\"..\"q4\" fine.\n\nValues arrays (32 each). Let me write them carefully.\n\nOrder: I'll list all class 1 then class 2.\n\ndiameter:\nDN50c1: 50; DN65c1: 65; DN80c1: 80; DN100c1: 100; DN125c1: 125; DN150c1: 150; DN200c1: 200; DN250c1: 250; DN300c1: 300; DN350c1: 350; DN400c1: 400; DN450c1: 450; DN500c1: 500; DN600c1: 600;\nDN50c2: 50; DN50RBc2: 50; DN65c2: 65; DN65RBc2: 65; DN80c2: 80; DN80RBc2: 80; DN100c2: 100; DN100RBc2: 100; DN125c2: 125; DN150c2: 150; DN200c2: 200; DN250c2: 250; DN300c2: 300; DN350c2: 350; DN400c2: 400; DN450c2: 450; DN500c2: 500; DN600c2: 600.\n\nq1:\nc1: 0.315, 0.5, 0.8, 1, 1.6, 3.9375, 6.25, 10, 15.625, 15.625, 25, 39.375, 39.375, 50.4\nc2: DN50 0.315, RB 0.158, DN65 0.5, RB 0.25, DN80 0.8, RB 0.4, DN100 1, RB 0.63, DN125 1.6, DN150 2.52, DN200 6.4, DN250 6.4, DN300 10, DN350 10, DN400 16, DN450 25.2, DN500 25.2, DN600 40.\n\nq2:\nc1: 0.504, 0.8, 1.28, 1.6, 2.56, 6.3, 10, 16, 25, 25, 40, 63, 63, 80.64\nc2: 0.504, 0.25, 0.8, 0.4, 1.28, 0.64, 1.6, 1.00 (1), 2.56, 4.032, 10.24, 10.24, 16, 16, 25.6, 40.32, 40.32, 64.\n\nq3:\nc1: 63, 100, 160, 250, 400, 630, 1000, 1600, 2500, 2500, 4000, 6300, 6300, 6300\nc2: 63, 63, 100, 100, 160, 160, 250, 250, 400, 630, 1600, 1600, 2500, 2500, 4000, 6300, 6300, 10000.\n\nq4:\nc1: 78.75, 125, 200, 312.5, 500, 787.5, 1250, 2000, 3125, 3125, 5000, 7875, 7875, 7875\nc2: 78.75, 78.75, 125, 125, 200, 200, 312.5, 312.5, 500, 787.5, 2000, 2000, 3125, 3125, 5000, 7875, 7875, 12500.\n\nratio_q3_q1:\nc1: 200, 200, 200, 250, 250, 160, 160, 160, 160, 160, 160, 160, 160, 125\nc2: 200, 400, 200, 400, 200, 400, 250, 400, 250, 250, 250, 250, 250, 250, 250, 250, 250, 250.\n\nmatrix_tables: include both tables fully with rows as objects. This duplicates model_level data but schema says preserve multi-dim tables. The example R137 does both. OK.\n\nfootnotes:\n- marker: \"*\", text: \"For the reduced bore (RB) version, the size mentioned is the process connection size. On the inscriptions of the water meter the reduced bore meter will be identified as “DNxxx RB”.\"\n\nShould rows in Table 3 RB entries have footnote markers? The schema for matrix_tables rows are plain objects; footnotes listed at table level. Fine.\n\nconfig_level: pressure_loss entries. Also note Table 1 says pressure loss class values depend on bore — config axis \"bore\". Include:\n- {attribute: \"pressure_loss_class\", axis: \"bore\", values: [{condition: \"full bore\", value: \"Δp 10\"}, {condition: \"reduced bore\", value: \"Δp 40\"}]}\n- {attribute: \"maximum_pressure_loss\", axis: \"bore\", values: [{condition: \"full bore\", value: 0.1, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}, {condition: \"reduced bore\", value: 0.4, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}]}\n\nWait \"Δp 10\" — no LaTeX, fine. Avoid \"Δ\"? It's a symbol, allowed (not LaTeX). Keep.\n\nAccuracy class values in models attributes: 1 or 2 (number).\n\nwater_temperature_class: value \"T50\"; add water_temperature_range {min 0.1, max 50} °C. The \"+0,1 °C / +50 °C\" is the class T50 range per R49 (T50: 0.1 to 50 °C). I'll include.\n\nAlso \"temperature_range_ambient\": -25 to +55 °C.\n\nNow the JSON. Should be parseable; no fences... The instruction: \"Return a single JSON object (no markdown fences, no commentary).\" Output raw JSON.\n\ntest_reports entries:\n1. {id: \"NMi-13200198-01\", date: \"2022-07-07\", pages: 29, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"} — hmm, decide role. Given uncertainty, \"test_report\". But it's likely the TER... The cert says \"represented by the sample(s) identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report\" and then lists associated reports. I'll mark the first as type_evaluation_report? Risk of fabrication. The instruction says don't fabricate fields not present. Role must be inferred. I'll use \"test_report\" for all seven — safe, since they're \"results of tests and examinations\" reports. Actually \"evaluation_report\" might be more accurate (\"tests and examinations\")... I'll use \"test_report\".\n\nHmm, one more thought: NMi-13200198-01 \"revision 1\" — includes revision. Schema test_reports has no revision field; id could include \" revision 1\"? I'll put id: \"NMi-13200198-01\" and add revision info in id? Better keep id clean; maybe include \"revision\": not in schema. Omit revision.\n\nDates: 7 July 2022 → \"2022-07-07\"; 4 July 2023 → \"2023-07-04\"; 17 April 2025 → \"2025-04-17\"; 31 July 2025 → \"2025-07-31\"; 3 October 2025 → \"2025-10-03\"; 3 October 2025 → \"2025-10-03\"; 23 December 2025 → \"2025-12-23\".\n\ndocumentation file: {id: \"T10554-7\", role: \"documentation_file\"}.\n\nCertificate number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03\". revision 11 — revision_history not provided; omit.\n\nLet me now also decide type_level accuracy_class: value \"1 and 2\" — string. Fine.\n\nmeasuring_principle value string.\n\nOutput size: model_level with 6 attributes × 32 values. Let's write compactly.\n\nAlso include \"meter_size\" as model_level attribute? Redundant with model_id; skip.\n\nWrite the JSON now.\n\nFor matrix_tables rows, keys: meter_size, diameter_inlet_outlet, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1.\n\nTable 1 name: \"General characteristics of the family of instruments with accuracy class 1 (Table 2)\".\n\nLet me double check numbers: DN150 c1 Q1 3,9375 → 3.9375. Yes (63/16). DN600 c1: 50,400 → 50.4; 80,640 → 80.64.\n\nNow assemble. I'll be careful with JSON validity — no trailing commas, no comments, proper escaping of quotes in footnote text (use \\\" for the inner quotes or use different quotes). Footnote text contains “DNxxx RB” with curly quotes — I can keep curly quotes, no escaping needed.\n\nBuild model_level values arrays.\n\nI'll write it out.\n\nOne consideration: models[] label field: e.g., label: \"DN50 reduced bore, accuracy class 2\" for RB. Full bore: label: \"DN50, accuracy class 1\". Provide attributes: {accuracy_class: 1, bore: \"full\"} / {accuracy_class: 2, bore: \"full\"} / {accuracy_class: 2, bore: \"reduced\"}.\n\nFinal JSON structure:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"4141724\",\n    \"page_total\": 7,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2026-07-28\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V.\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\",\n    \"phone\": \"+31 88 636 2332\",\n    \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n    \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\"name\": \"Badger Meter, Inc.\", \"address_lines\": [\"4545 West Brown Deer Road\", \"Milwaukee, WI 53224\", \"United States of America\"]}\n  ],\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\"name\": \"Badger Meter Czech Republic s.r.o.\", \"address_lines\": [\"Maříkova 2082/26\", \"62100 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"]}\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"M5000\"],\n    \"description\": \"An electromagnetic water meter\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"M5000\",\n    \"models\": [ 32 entries ]\n  },\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {...},\n    \"model_level\": [...],\n    \"config_level\": [...]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...2 tables...],\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49-1\",\n    \"edition\": 2013,\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]\n  },\n  \"test_reports\": [...8...],\n  \"footnotes\": [{\"marker\": \"*\", \"text\": \"...\"}]\n}\n\nfamily_name: is \"M5000\" an explicit family name? The cert says \"family of instruments\" but no explicit family name. Rule: \"explicit family name if any, else null\". M5000 is the type designation covering the family. I'll set family_name: \"M5000\". Hmm, or null. The type M5000 identifies the family. I'll use \"M5000\".\n\nNow write all 32 models.\n\nmodels:\n1 {\"model_id\": \"DN50 (class 1)\", \"label\": \"DN50, accuracy class 1, full bore\", \"attributes\": {\"accuracy_class\": 1, \"bore\": \"full\"}}\n2 DN65 (class 1)\n3 DN80 (class 1)\n4 DN100 (class 1)\n5 DN125 (class 1)\n6 DN150 (class 1)\n7 DN200 (class 1)\n8 DN250 (class 1)\n9 DN300 (class 1)\n10 DN350 (class 1)\n11 DN400 (class 1)\n12 DN450 (class 1)\n13 DN500 (class 1)\n14 DN600 (class 1)\n15 DN50 (class 2)\n16 DN50 RB (class 2) — label \"DN50 reduced bore (DN50 RB), accuracy class 2\", attributes bore \"reduced\"\n17 DN65 (class 2)\n18 DN65 RB (class 2)\n19 DN80 (class 2)\n20 DN80 RB (class 2)\n21 DN100 (class 2)\n22 DN100 RB (class 2)\n23 DN125 (class 2)\n24 DN150 (class 2)\n25 DN200 (class 2)\n26 DN250 (class 2)\n27 DN300 (class 2)\n28 DN350 (class 2)\n29 DN400 (class 2)\n30 DN450 (class 2)\n31 DN500 (class 2)\n32 DN600 (class 2)\n\nmodel_level arrays (order same as models list).\n\nNow, produce final JSON. Watch quoting of \"DNxxx RB\" inside footnote — curly quotes fine.\n\nLet me write the full JSON carefully.",
